
Module V: String Methods & Formatting
1. Introduction to String Methods
Module V mein humne Python ke string methods aur string formatting ke baare mein seekha.

String methods ka use text ko:

Clean karne


Change karne


Search karne


Split karne


Combine karne


Proper format mein display karne


ke liye kiya jaata hai.

Good formatting ka purpose sirf decoration nahi hai. A well-formatted report easy to read, understand and verify hoti hai.


2. Why Formatting is Important
Agar report mein data ek messy heap ki tarah ho:

PE-0137 25.02 ACCEPT PE-0138 26.40 REJECT


to supervisor ke liye read karna difficult ho sakta hai.

Formatted report:

Part        Diameter    Status

PE-0137       25.02    ACCEPT

PE-0138       26.40    REJECT


zyada clear aur professional hoti hai.

What I learned
Formatting is not just decoration. Legibility is important because people need to understand data quickly and correctly.


3. .strip() Method
.strip() string ke beginning aur end se extra whitespace remove karta hai.

Example:

field = "   PE-0137   "

clean = field.strip()


print(clean)


Output:

PE-0137


Shop-floor use
Log files aur input fields mein unwanted spaces remove karne ke liye .strip() bahut useful hai.


4. .upper() and .lower()
.upper() text ko uppercase mein convert karta hai.

shift = "night"

print(shift.upper())


Output:

NIGHT


.lower() text ko lowercase mein convert karta hai.

shift = "NIGHT"

print(shift.lower())


Output:

night


Why useful?
"night", "Night" aur "NIGHT" ko same form mein convert karke comparison easy ho jaata hai.


5. .replace() Method
.replace(a, b) string mein ek value ko doosri value se replace karta hai.

Example:

value = "25,02"

value = value.replace(",", ".")


print(value)


Output:

25.02


Shop-floor use
Stray characters ya incorrect separators ko clean karne ke liye .replace() useful hai.


6. .startswith() Method
.startswith() check karta hai ki string kisi particular text se start hoti hai ya nahi.

Example:

part = "PE-0137"


print(part.startswith("PE-"))


Output:

True


Shop-floor use
Iska use particular part codes ko filter karne ke liye kiya ja sakta hai.


7. .split() Method
.split() ek string ko multiple parts mein divide karke list banata hai.

Example:

row = "PE-0137,25.02,ACCEPT"


fields = row.split(",")


print(fields)


Output:

['PE-0137', '25.02', 'ACCEPT']


What I learned
.split() ka use CSV ya log lines ko individual fields mein break karne ke liye hota hai.


8. .join() Method
.join() list ke multiple strings ko ek single string mein combine karta hai.

Example:

fields = ["PE-0137", "25.02", "ACCEPT"]


row = ",".join(fields)


print(row)


Output:

PE-0137,25.02,ACCEPT


Important
.split() aur .join() ek pair ki tarah kaam karte hain.

split → text ko parts mein todta hai

join → parts ko text mein jodta hai


9. Split and Join Together
Example:

fields = ["PE-0137", "25.02", "ACCEPT"]


row = ",".join(fields)


back = row.split(",")


print(row)

print(back)


Output:

PE-0137,25.02,ACCEPT

['PE-0137', '25.02', 'ACCEPT']


Easy way to remember
String → split() → List

List → join() → String


Ye concept future mein CSV files create aur read karne mein important hoga.


10. .zfill() Method
.zfill(n) string ke beginning mein zeros add karta hai jab tak required length complete na ho.

Example:

part = "7"


print(part.zfill(4))


Output:

0007


Shop-floor use
Part numbers aur machine IDs ko fixed format mein display karne ke liye .zfill() useful hai.


11. .count() and .find()
.count()
.count() batata hai ki koi text kitni baar appear hua.

text = "PE-0137,PE-0138,PE-0137"


print(text.count("PE-"))


Output:

3


.find()
.find() batata hai ki particular text pehli baar kis position par mila.

text = "PE-0137"


print(text.find("-"))


Ye - ki position return karega.

What I learned
Ye methods quick checks aur parsing se pehle useful hote hain.


12. Introduction to f-Strings
f-string Python mein strings ke andar variables aur expressions ko easily insert karne ka method hai.

Example:

tag = "L-01"

rpm = 1500


print(f"Machine {tag} is running at {rpm} rpm")


Output:

Machine L-01 is running at 1500 rpm


f-strings formatting ko simple aur readable banate hain.


13. Format Specification
Format specification ka use values ko specific width, alignment aur decimal places ke saath display karne ke liye hota hai.

Example:

f"{dia:>10.2f}"


Isko parts mein samajh sakte hain:

{dia:>10.2f}

      │ │  │

      │ │  └── f = fixed-point number

      │ └───── .2 = 2 decimal places

      └─────── >10 = right aligned, 10 characters wide


Example:

dia = 25.02


print(f"{dia:>10.2f}")


Output mein number fixed width aur 2 decimal places ke saath appear hoga.


14. Alignment, Width and Number Formatting
Different format specifications:

f"{tag:<12}"


< means left alignment.

f"{n:>6}"


> means right alignment.

f"{name:^12}"


^ means centre alignment.

Other useful examples
f"{total:>12,.2f}"


This adds:

Right alignment


Width of 12


Comma separator


2 decimal places


Example:

1,240.50


For percentages:

f"{pct:>6.1%}"


If:

pct = 0.9375


Output:

93.8%



15. What We Learned + Main Idea
From this topic, I learned:

String methods help us clean and process text.


.strip() removes extra spaces.


.upper() and .lower() change letter case.


.replace() replaces unwanted characters or text.


.startswith() checks how a string begins.


.split() converts text into a list of fields.


.join() combines a list into a string.


.zfill() adds leading zeros.


.count() counts occurrences of text.


.find() finds the position of text.


f-strings make formatted output easy.


Format specifications control alignment, width and decimals.


Numbers are usually right-aligned so decimal places and magnitudes are easier to compare.


Labels are usually left-aligned so they are easier to scan.


Proper formatting turns raw data into a clear and readable report.


Main Idea
String Methods → Clean and Process Data

        ↓

split() → Break text into fields

join()  → Build text from fields

        ↓

f-strings → Format the final output

        ↓

Readable and Professional Report


In short: clean the data, format the data, and present the data so that a person can understand it quickly.
